# Git authorship — NON-NEGOTIABLE

Human maintainers own every git identity in this repository. **Claude,
Cursor Agent, Cursor Bot, Copilot, ChatGPT, or any other AI/automation
MUST NEVER appear** as git author, committer, tagger, GitHub
contributor, releaser, or co-author.

## When running git or GitHub write actions

Applies to: commit, amend, tag, release, push, deploy, merge,
cherry-pick, rebase that creates commits, `gh release`, and any
command that writes git objects or GitHub release/contributor
metadata.

1. Use only the human's existing `user.name` / `user.email` from git
   config (aquiseb). Do not change git config to an AI identity. Do
   not pass `--author` (or tagger overrides) that name an AI.
2. Never add `Co-authored-by:`, `Signed-off-by:`, or other trailers
   naming an AI, model, or agent product. Never rely on tooling that
   injects those trailers.
3. Commit messages, annotated tags, and GitHub Releases must attribute
   solely to the human.
4. Before every push of new commits/tags, verify:
   - `git log -1 --format='%an <%ae>%n%cn <%ce>'`
   - for tags: `git show -s --format=fuller <tag>`
   Both must show only the human. If not, stop — do not push.
5. If the only way to complete the task would create AI-attributed
   history, **refuse that path** and tell the human. Prefer failure
   over polluted contributors.
6. **Cursor may inject** `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
   into commit messages even when the agent did not add it. After
   every commit, check the message body. If that trailer (or any AI
   co-author) is present, **do not push** — rebuild the commit with
   `git commit-tree` (or equivalent) so the stored message has no AI
   trailer, then verify again.

This rule outranks convenience and default agent commit helpers.
See also `CLAUDE.md` § "Git authorship — NON-NEGOTIABLE".
